A||||24||80's BOY||Running 50/50 Av Gas with pump gas||||5||
Z||000000||80's BOY||09-26-2000||02:28 PM||||some mustang guys were doing this the other night, and one guy had about 4 gallons of aviation fuel left in his gas can. i put it in with my 1/4 tank for the 3.1 engine and my ass-o-meter seemed to tell the difference, but does it really help?? sometimes i buy the "outlaw" octane boost and it's 4 or 5 bucks for a bottle. now aviation fuel is really dry and they recommend mixing it 50/50 with a full tank. any input??||||reg||5
Z||000001||pollock||09-26-2000||04:18 PM||||Couple thoughts... (not much scientific basis)<P>Higher octane means the fuel ignites more slowly.  To me, this means you want to run the lowest octane gas you can, avoiding detonation.<P>I've heard that octane boosters thicken the fuel.  A thicker liquid will not have the same flow characteristics, so this just introduces another variable.  <P>I say run on 89 if you can, or 93 if you have to.  Screw the avgas.  That's for guys with 13:1 compression.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||09-26-2000||04:29 PM||||The high octane of AV fuel is also for highly loaded engines at low RPM's.||||reg||1
Z||000003||gixxer||09-26-2000||08:11 PM||||You have to watch out what fuel you get.  At a general aviation airport you typically get a pick:<BR>-80 octane leaded (red tinted) It may be no-lead 'tho, I don't remember.<BR>-100 octane leaded (green)<BR>-100 octane low lead (blue)<BR>Seems that most places carry the 100 LL.<BR>I think these are rated using the old optimistic grading system.<BR>The fuel is usually fairly carefully refined compared to auto gas, but it will pick up water in the underground tanks it's stored in. That's why pilots spend some time during pre-flight checking for water by sampling the fuel out of drains in the wings and the engine bay. A Cessna 172 for example has three places to check. <BR>Oh yeah, if the fuel is clear, it's jet fuel.<BR>Your engine will quit if you use it(kerosene). And leaded fuel wrecks yer catalytic converter.<BR>If you see a difference using the 100 octane stuff, your ignition is probably over advanced or the compression is too high.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||80's BOY||09-26-2000||08:39 PM||||What catalytic converter??????||||reg||10
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||09-26-2000||08:50 PM||||What kind of Fiero do you have?<BR>I think 85 v6 cars had knock sensors and higher octane would work since they have more aggresive timing that would show more power with higher octane. AvGas may be a little much though.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||09-26-2000||09:11 PM||||Iv ran 98 oct. gas in my Fiero and it ran a lot better. It was racing fuel, not AV fuel though. 80's Boy, you dont have a cat? I have a clogged cat now, and was wondering should I replace it or eliminate it. I could get by with not having one here, but what does it do to gas milage? I am currently getting 10-11 mpg at the most, so anything would help. Does it improve performance? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000007||capt_ron||09-26-2000||10:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gixxer:<BR><B>You have to watch out what fuel you get.  At a general aviation airport you typically get a pick:<BR>-80 octane leaded (red tinted) It may be no-lead 'tho, I don't remember.<BR>-100 octane leaded (green)<BR>-100 octane low lead (blue)<BR>Seems that most places carry the 100 LL.<BR>I think these are rated using the old optimistic grading system.<BR>The fuel is usually fairly carefully refined compared to auto gas, but it will pick up water in the underground tanks it's stored in. That's why pilots spend some time during pre-flight checking for water by sampling the fuel out of drains in the wings and the engine bay. A Cessna 172 for example has three places to check. <BR>Oh yeah, if the fuel is clear, it's jet fuel.<BR>Your engine will quit if you use it(kerosene). And leaded fuel wrecks yer catalytic converter.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The Red 80 octane avgas was leaded and hasn't been sold in many years. The replacement 100LL avgas is blue and still contains lead but not near as much as the 80. I'm not familiar with the green 100 octane however a new 82 octane avgas is being marketed but I haven't seen any of it either and I'm not sure what color. It may be red. As for water in the fuel, that usually comes from worn-out seals in the plane's fuel caps or condensation in the fuel tanks. That's why we top them off, right? Checking for jet fuel is simple even if it's mixed with avgas, pour a little on a white piece of paper. If it evaporates, fine, if not then you best hope that your winged wonder has a gas turbine in it. One of the biggest differences between auto gas and avgas is the chemical make up. Auto gas will get old and form varnish, avgas does not age. Avgas is great for portable generators and occasional use equipment for this reason. One word of caution though, using other than the recommended fuel can cause problems with O-rings and other components in the fuel system. Besides a whiff of burnt avgas is better than the finest french perfume, just wish it didn't cost so much.<P>Running 100LL in a Fiero is not bad but then with the gas already in the tank the octane was cut back a bit depending on the octane of the gas already in the tank. If you had 87 in the tank then the blend would have been around 93.5, 89= 95.5, and 93= 96.5 octane. These are just approximate but close. BTW, there is no midrange 89 octane gasoline, it is a 40/60 blend of premium(93) and regular unleaded(87). Some gas stations have blender pumps and only have premium and regular storage tanks. Others have tanks for all three 87,89,&93, the mid(89) is mixed when the truck is loaded. For 1000 gal of mid 400 gal of premium is loaded first then 600 gal of regular. This is done automatically at the loading rack. Ammoco Ultimate(93) is in a different storage tank and has a clear appearance.<P>All these posts are good infomation, keep in mind that most Lycoming and Continental engines used in aircraft have a compression ratio of 8.5:1.<BR>The big radial engines like the 2800hp Pratt and Whitney and the 2600hp Wright have CR's of 6.75:1 and 6.70:1 respectively. However they are certified to use either 100LL or 130 octane avgas. (The 130 is purple I believe) They also use ADI, Anti Detonation Injection to increase horsepower for take-off. <P>Sorry for the long post but I get carried away and have the data disks for all this stuff. Hope that I did stray too far from the thread.<P><P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.||||reg||1
Z||000008||capt_ron||09-26-2000||10:51 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Iv ran 98 oct. gas in my Fiero and it ran a lot better. It was racing fuel, not AV fuel though. 80's Boy, you dont have a cat? I have a clogged cat now, and was wondering should I replace it or eliminate it. I could get by with not having one here, but what does it do to gas milage? I am currently getting 10-11 mpg at the most, so anything would help. Does it improve performance? Thanks<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sean,<BR>I removed the cat from my V-6 when it plugged and put a piece of flex pipe in to get the car home. Took longer to put it on the ramps than to sawzall the old cat out and put the flex in with 2 clamps. I left the flex for about a month but the exhaust was too loud so I bought a round muffler off a bargin table for $10 and welded it in place. Now the exhaust noise is normal and the muffler fits in the cat area perfectly. <P>My car gets about 25mpg around town. I know this because I have threatened my G/F so much about running the car out of gas that she sets the trip odometer every time she fills the car up. When it gets to 250 miles she puts in about 10 gallons to fill it up again. I can't imagine burning the amount of gas in a Fiero that you say. But then again maybe you have one of those new Fiero SUV's. If your cat is that plugged the engine would barely run, been there, done that.<BR>As for improving the performance, you bet. <BR>Good Luck  <P><BR>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.<p>[This message has been edited by capt_ron (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||09-26-2000||11:16 PM||||Pollock:<BR>     High octane fuel does not burn more slowly. It burns at the same speed as lower octane, but is not as prone to explode.<P>Pontiaddict:<BR>     No V6 Fieros ever came with knock sensors ('87,'88 DIS Iron Ducks might have? I don't know for sure). Since there is no feedback mechanism, the computer can not control timing.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||TOM||09-27-2000||01:27 AM||||I run av gas in my mustang and in my truck, Neither of which has a converter. I run it staright. I can also get great deals on it. Usually free. But even when bought it is sometimes cheaper than pump gas with the way prices are going.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||09-27-2000||07:44 AM||||I think I might remove the cat and replace it with Rodney's cat elimanation pipe. If I think its too loud and I probally go back to a high flow cat. or maybe even a muffler. The reason I ask is because someone said they lost torque when they removed the cat. but they also said that the new exhaust sound was worth it. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||80's BOY||09-27-2000||09:26 AM||||seanh, you're right about the torque loss. i lost alot when had my exhaust done. i replaced the cat with a glasspack tube muffler, and installed holley resonated tips. it sounds like a ferrari, but now i'm dead in the hole at a green light. a little give and take i guess. i don't regret it.||||reg||14
Z||000013||pollock||09-27-2000||09:32 AM||||when it comes to fuel, what's the difference between "ignites slowly" and "less prone to explode?"||||reg||1
