Z||000015||EdsB52||09-27-2000||10:39 AM||||I'm surprised that Capt Ron didn't straighten out one point, with all that awesome information he has. The point given earlier was that someone asserted that if fuel is clear, it's jet fuel. If you mix different octanes of avgas (which are colored), the fuel also becomes clear.<P>As far as the "explosion of fuel," it's been explained to me that lower octanes of fuel tend to ignite from one molecule to the rest of the molecules, while higher octanes ignite in a chain reactin sense, creating a slower but more consistent burn. This is kind of a laymans approach, maybe someone can expound. Hey Ron, what do you do in aviation? I'm an A&P, IA, Private Pilot, and an FCC - GROL. I have done it all my life and am tired of it as a profession. I'm going to college to one day become an attorney! Sounds like you have vast knowledge in aviation. Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Seanh||09-27-2000||05:23 PM||||So its that big of a loss? Maybe I might just replace it with a new cat. I like to race and I need all the power I can get. How much more power do the HO V6 put out. I have an 86GT with a V6, I decided against the N* because all the other mods you have to do to the car. I would like to get a new engine, but not change the car all that much. Any Ideas on where I could get my hands on a HO V6? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000017||Sillhouette.com||09-27-2000||05:34 PM||||Some clarifications!<P>The aviation 130 octane fuel is green coloured not blue.  The only one that is blue is the 100 LL.  There will be no more 130 or 87 octane avgas (green and red).  They went over to 100LL only for piston driven aircraft.  <P>The higher the octane the higher the explosion.  That is one way to get yourself more power.  That is why you felt more power using 100LL.  The only consideration is the LL part of it that will plug your catalic converter.  <P>Jet fuel is like diesel.  Jet engines are designed to have a steady burn not explosion.  At times when emergency fuel is required manufacturers allow us (pilots) to uplift aviation gasoline and then the lowest octane possible.  Turbine and Jet engines do not explode they burn steady.  <P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000018||mrfixit58||09-27-2000||05:35 PM||||pollock - Running higher octane fuel allow you to run more timing. Running more timing allow more fuel to burn per cylindar fill (improved efficiency). The more fuel that burns, the more powerthe motor makes.<P>Running too low of octane and too much timing will rob your engine of power.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000019||Standard||09-27-2000||06:27 PM||||Sean - You already have an HO v6.  All fiero v6's had the High Output version.  <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||09-28-2000||07:19 AM||||Ok, I have just been seeing some picutres of a kinda yellowish-greenish engine that said HO V6. Iv seen it in more then one car, so I just figured it might have been on a certain year. BTW, any clue where I could get that lettering that they have on the engine, kinda raised gold letters?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000021||ThatRickGuy||09-28-2000||06:24 PM||||I've gotten into the Octane debate too many times, but I'll jsut throw this out here. The higher the octane rating, the more oxygen that is already in the fuel, which means at 93 oct vs 89 oct you have to pull in more fuel to maintain the optimal air:fuel ratio (16:1 err something like that). 89 oct and 93 will burn at the same speed, but since at 93 there is more fuel to burn, it will burn longer, this allows you to move the timing forward (advanced) to get more fire time on the power stroke. I can't remeber the whole theory behind low gas/to much advance and knocking at the moment (kinda tired) but basically, if you have a stock 2.8 v6, running high octane gas won't do much for you. Now if you convert it to a 3.2 w/ flat top pistons and shave the deck w/ alluminum heads, you'll be running arround 13:1 compression and you'll need 100 LL just to get it to run.<P>as for the exhaust/power posts earlier. opening the exhaust will decrease your torque. Torque is some screwy beast that likes back preasure (even I don't know the physics behind that one!) with out it, Torque get's pissy. Horsepower on the other hand, likes to breath, a big ol' intake, headers, open exhaust, large valves, etc... So by opening your exhaust up, you're going to lose some of that off the line seat of your pants feeling, but out on the open highway, you'll have more power on the top end. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||lowCG||10-01-2000||12:09 AM||||I,m pretty sure that AV gas is a different weight than normal fuel,which will change the mixture.||||reg||1
Z||000023||FIEROCIOUS||10-05-2000||05:05 AM||||Wow, No one has even the slightest idea about octane or Avgas. You all are confuseing the heck out of me and I know a great deal about gas! <P>First off, the higher the octane rateing the slower it burns not faster!  You will NOT gain any more power from a higher octane than what is required to keep your car from detonateing! Period! NO GAINS! If your car is detonateing (pinging) then you may benifite from a higher octane level. Now as said before why would you want to slow the combution process? You wouldn't! You want the flame front to travel as rapidly as possible at a steady pressure rate. Detonation causes pressure spikes and is detrimental to engine performance and longevity! Power is a measurement of work devided by time. The only way you will get more (power) is if you get more mecanical advantage and/or work done faster!<BR>What this means to you and me is you want the flame front from the sparkplug to act upon the cylinder quicker and harder the way this is done is by heat the higher the heat produced and "used" the more power. <P>Bottom line use the lowest octane rateing your car can use without detonation. This will vary from winter to summer (lower octain in the winter higher in the summer) gas companies do the math for you and use differant gas mixtures to suit the season ie. they put alcohol (oxegenated gas) for winter use. This a: helps keep water out of the gas and raises the ron rateing so they can use a lower gas rateing.<P>Ron: research octane number<BR>Mon: Motor octane number<P>Ron rateings are set at specific variables<BR>Mon rateings are set at motor variables<P>what you read at the pump is an average of the two RON/MON X2 =Advertized octain<P>Avgas should not be used in cars! It has a low specific gravity that is meant for high altitudes! (Planes Fly!) If you live above 5000 ft. you can consider Avgas but only at 10% volume!<P>If you want to raise the octane of your current gas use tuelene, or isopropyl alcohol (rubbing alcohol) mixed with trans fluid- (cleaner/detergent) and or mineral spirits.<BR>Again use only in volumes less than 10%!!! <BR>I will not be held responceable if someone try this and F***s up their engine!  I do not recomend any octane than what is currently available at the pump!<P>[This message has been edited by FIEROCIOUS (edited 10-05-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by FIEROCIOUS (edited 10-05-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000024||lowCG||10-07-2000||11:38 AM||||Better yet,don't even risk destroying your fuel pump and injectors with industrial solvents used as additives and stick with pump gas,unless you really know about the effects.<BR>||||reg||1
